WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.880
With so many Spider-Man movies out there,
there are a lot of Spider-Man villains.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.860
We're taking a look back at the many, many
supervillains that Spidey's battled against.

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:11.520
Beware: some gigantic spoilers ahead.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.840
Stop the video now if you're not up to date
on every Spider-movie!

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:20.340
The Amazing Spider-Man 2 is, even more than
Spider-Man 3 before it, an overstuffed movie

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:25.130
that banks on interconnectivity to help drive
excitement for a sequel that ultimately never

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:25.880
came.

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:29.840
At the time, Sony Pictures was attempting
to push ahead with its own cinematic universe

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.320
with Spider-Man at the heart.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:34.720
When it didn't work, we ended up with Spider-Man
in the MCU.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.320
Watching The Amazing Spider-Man 2 today is
mostly an amusing exercise in seeing someone

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:42.000
try to build a universe that never quite comes
together.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.480
Rhino is, sadly, a victim of this crumbling
effort.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:50.399
The film had Paul Giamatti playing a Russian
gangster in a mechanical rhino suit, and the

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:54.240
actor clearly relished chewing up as much
scenery as he possibly could in the role.

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.940
Sadly, he only appears in a couple of scenes.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:01.280
He might've had more to do in a hypothetical
sequel, but here he's largely an example of

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:02.620
a wasted opportunity.

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:07.040
The version of the Green Goblin who shows
up in The Amazing Spider-Man 2 is a perfect

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:12.400
example of the film pushing ahead for universe-building
while sacrificing any real sense of long term

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:13.300
storytelling.

00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:17.479
We've never met this version of Harry Osborn
before, but we're told he's got a connection

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:19.080
to Peter going back years.

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:22.860
We haven't seen the toll his genetic illness
is taking on him, but we're told it's bad

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:24.040
and we should care.

00:01:24.040 --> 00:01:28.659
We haven't seen any real development in terms
of Green Goblin tech, but we're shown it all

00:01:28.660 --> 00:01:30.860
packaged and ready for Harry to dive in.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:33.900
It's a whole lot of telling without any emotional
weight.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:38.000
This lack of real investment makes this the
most forgettable version of Green Goblin on

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:39.380
the big screen so far.

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:45.040
Making Electro the primary villain of The
Amazing Spider-Man 2 was a good idea on paper.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:49.579
He's got a cool powers that can manifest themselves
in interesting ways visually, and he was played

00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:52.760
by Jamie Foxx, who offered up a new version
of the character.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:55.080
Unfortunately, it doesn't quite work.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:59.420
Foxx seemed to do the best he could with the
material he had, but the character's motivations

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:02.760
get lost in the shuffle of everything else
the film is trying to do.

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:07.399
By the time Electro is somehow actually playing
music with his powers at the end of the film,

00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:09.740
he's lost any goodwill he had before.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.760
"I hate this song."

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:24.040
Venom is a complicated character, and introducing
him into any film as a Spider-Man antagonist

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:26.600
has the potential to be very tricky business.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:31.700
The way Spider-Man 3 does it works fine as
a catalyst for the film's climax, but doesn't

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:36.160
really let audiences get to know this particular
version of the villain and how he works.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:41.120
There's no time to dig into what the relationship
to the symbiote is, or exactly why Eddie Brock

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.060
gives in so quickly, or if there's even any
real humanity left in him.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:49.200
This version of Venom is pretty much just
a fight scene, and that's a shame.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:55.900
Spider-Man: Homecoming, the first solo Spider-Man
adventure in the Marvel Cinematic Universe,

00:02:55.900 --> 00:03:00.230
is a great example of a film that knows how
to juggle multiple villains without overwhelming

00:03:00.230 --> 00:03:01.040
the piece.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:05.680
While Vulture does the heavy lifting as the
film's chief bad guy, his crew back at headquarters

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:08.940
fits into the story in an organic and entertaining
way.

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:12.799
However, the Tinkerer doesn't rank higher
on this list because he doesn't have that

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:14.160
much to do in the film.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:18.260
He's the guy in the chair, manipulating tech
and communications behind the scenes while

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:19.720
the Vulture does the fighting.

00:03:19.720 --> 00:03:24.099
Because of that, he's not a heavy hitter within
the film, but he's certainly entertaining

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:25.360
in the space he's given.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:30.120
Homecoming manages to breathe real life into
him by making him a nosy, strange guy who's

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.950
just happy to have a gig where he can be a
huge tech nerd.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:35.500
"What'd I tell you about looking at my phone?"

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:37.360
"Oh, sorry, you left it out.

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.620
You know I'm a curious person by nature."

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:44.300
The economy of storytelling in Homecoming
is so great that the film manages to fit in

00:03:44.300 --> 00:03:46.840
not one, but two versions of the Shocker.

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:51.280
In the film, he's an employee of the Vulture
who's gifted with gauntlets that emit tremendous

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:54.060
energy when they make contact with someone
or something.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:57.380
It's enough to make the character stand out
in his few fight scenes.

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:01.519
The first Shocker relishes the idea that he
can have a self-given code name and go around

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:03.380
punching things with his new toys.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:08.420
"You're up there wearing that goofy thing,
lighting up cars, calling yourself 'the Shocker.

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:10.340
I'm the Shocker, I shock people!'

00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:11.660
What is this, pro wrestling?"

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:13.020
"Eh, whatever old man."

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:17.420
When the second Shocker gets hold of the gauntlets,
things become much more businesslike, and

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:21.480
the deliberate, calculated way in which he
tries to pick apart Peter Parker is actually

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:22.530
rather chilling.

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:26.640
Together, they present a portrait of a minor
supervillain that adds plenty to the film

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:29.020
while never getting in the way of Vulture's
spotlight.

00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:33.940
The Amazing Spider-Man wisely started its
new Spidey universe by picking a villain who'd

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:38.160
only been hinted at in the Sam Raimi films
in the form of a supporting character.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:42.460
We'd seen Dr. Curt Connors before, but we'd
never seen him transformed into a monster

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:45.300
by his own scientific hubris and desperation.

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:49.320
The fact that the character was in the Raimi
films created a sense of excitement among

00:04:49.320 --> 00:04:52.780
fans who were finally going to get to see
the Lizard, while never quite feeling like

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:55.570
the new film was retracing the steps of the
old ones.

00:04:55.570 --> 00:05:00.180
In The Amazing Spider-Man, Dr. Connors takes
on a certain kind of quiet strangeness that

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:01.960
permeates the early scenes.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:06.260
You get the sense that he's a man who's thought
far too much about things he probably shouldn't

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:07.420
be tinkering with.

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:12.340
As his slow transformation begins, the character
becomes even stranger, creating a villain

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:14.420
who's both threatening and sympathetic.

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:22.800
The decision to make The Sandman a major villain
in Spider-Man 3 is an interesting one, particularly

00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:26.280
when you consider all the other things the
film is trying to do with Peter Parker's emotional

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:27.160
journey.

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:32.100
The decision to make him the guy who killed
Uncle Ben offscreen in the first film complicates

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:35.960
this in ways that neither the film nor the
Spider-Man mythos really need.

00:05:35.960 --> 00:05:40.300
Despite this, this version of the Sandman
works because of the parallels the film draws

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:41.600
between him and Peter.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:46.300
They're both men with strange powers they
didn't ask for, pushed into darkness by circumstances

00:05:46.310 --> 00:05:50.150
beyond their control and hoping to atone for
their past mistakes.

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:54.050
Thomas Haden Church plays the character with
real heart, and his final scene is one of

00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:56.360
the most powerful moments in a flawed film.

00:05:57.420 --> 00:05:59.480
"I'm not asking you to forgive me.

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:02.680
I just want you to understand."

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:07.640
Spider-Man 3 could have been a film entirely
about the rise of a second Green Goblin in

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:11.870
parallel to Peter Parker wrestling with the
Venom symbiote, and it would have still been

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:13.000
a jam-packed movie.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:17.000
As it is, we got a film that attempted to
juggle three different villains and cram them

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:21.690
all together into a final fight scene, so
Harry Osborn's final journey in the Spider-Man

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:23.540
trilogy gets crammed in, too.

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:28.340
Still, Harry's decision to become the second
Green Goblin in an effort to avenge his father,

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:31.220
and then to seek heroic redemption, is a rewarding
one.

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:35.420
It doesn't always work, particularly when
Harry has to turn on a dime between hero and

00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:40.800
villain in various scenes, but the two preceding
films add emotional weight to a weaker script.

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:45.740
Willem Dafoe is having so much fun in the
role of Green Goblin, years before character

00:06:45.740 --> 00:06:50.350
actors would begin competing for supervillain
roles left and right, that his sense of instant

00:06:50.350 --> 00:06:53.780
joy at being immersed in the comic book world
is infectious.

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:58.520
Dafoe has to transform into full supervillain
mode almost instantly, and somehow, he brings

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:00.020
it to life flawlessly.

00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:02.520
"What do you want?"

00:07:03.320 --> 00:07:07.960
"To say what you won't, to do what you can't."

00:07:08.380 --> 00:07:13.120
He does so even in spite of the film's shortcomings
in its treatment of the character, something

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:15.980
that's particularly obvious in the costume
design.

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:20.400
The decision to forgo an expressive face for
the character and instead feature a fang-bearing

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:22.940
mask might have made sense in 2002.

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:27.380
Unfortunately, watching the film today, it
makes the scenes with Spider-Man and the Goblin

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:30.360
look a bit too much like action figures yelling
at each other.

00:07:30.360 --> 00:07:33.900
Even in those moments, though, Dafoe's voice
carries the performance.

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:38.760
If you didn't watch the intro, this is where
the big spoilers happen, so turn back now!

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:43.620
Spider-Man: Far from Home had to follow a
lot of different, very tough acts, just one

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:46.200
of which was Michael Keaton's performance
as the Vulture.

00:07:46.200 --> 00:07:50.900
The decision to make the next villain Mysterio,
another newcomer to live-action Spider-Man,

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:52.080
was a welcome one.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:56.820
Even better, fans seemed to immediately support
Jake Gyllenhaal's casting as Mysterio, a guy

00:07:56.820 --> 00:07:59.880
who creates illusions to disguise his true
intentions.

00:07:59.880 --> 00:08:03.800
Gyllenhaal gets a lot of meaty stuff to play
with in the film, from the idea that he first

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:08.380
presents himself as a hero from another dimension
to the revelation that he's a scorned former

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:10.120
employee of Tony Stark.

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:15.020
The early, more heroic scenes work great,
but the turn is where Gyllenhaal really shines.

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:19.580
Once the gloves are off and he's in full supervillain
mode, he's having the time of his life, and

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:20.500
so are we.

00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:24.800
He doesn't quite rise to the heights of the
very best live-action Spider-Man villains,

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:29.610
but he's very close, so much so that even
after Far From Home you can't help but feel

00:08:29.610 --> 00:08:30.920
hungry for more of him.

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:37.820
With a few notable exceptions, Sam Raimi's
Spider-Man films are often defined by their

00:08:37.820 --> 00:08:41.740
aesthetic and structural devotion to the Marvel
Comics book they're based on.

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:46.080
The key to the best of these films is the
way in which they know exactly how to deviate,

00:08:46.080 --> 00:08:46.940
and when.

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:50.940
With Spider-Man 2, we get the best of both
worlds in Doctor Octopus.

00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:55.460
He's a guy who looks and moves exactly how
we'd expect the comic book version to move,

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:59.760
but he has the added gravitas of a good man
corrupted by his own creation.

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:05.860
Alfred Molina is flawless as Dr. Otto Octavius,
a scientist who's hoping to change the world

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:10.710
before his brain is infected by the artificial
intelligence in his robotic extra arms after

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:12.140
a horrible accident.

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:16.860
Riddled with guilt and grief, Molina plays
the disintegration of the character perfectly,

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:21.200
then manages to guide him back to redemption
with the same sense of vulnerability and delicate

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:22.540
emotional resonance.

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:25.360
It's a great performance in a great comic
book movie.

00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:30.320
Sometimes Spider-Man stories are at their
very best when they take a very simple, easy-to-follow

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:34.520
metaphor and translate it into the big, bold
strokes of a superhero story.

00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:38.740
Spider-Man: Homecoming is a story about Peter
Parker finding out his girlfriend's dad is

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:42.220
a jerk, and the jerk is played to perfection
by Michael Keaton.

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:46.260
There's so much to love about the Vulture
in Homecoming, from the look of the character

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:50.360
to the way Keaton plays him as a working class
guy who got tired of being pushed around.

00:09:50.360 --> 00:09:55.180
There's no nonsense here, but there are tricky
emotional turns to make, and Keaton manages

00:09:55.180 --> 00:09:58.400
them all by being equal parts charming and
terrifying.

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:02.380
The scene in which he has a private moment
with Peter in his car is among the best moments

00:10:02.380 --> 00:10:04.000
in any Spider-Man film.

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:05.480
"Hey.

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:09.080
I just saved your life.

00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:11.120
Now what do you say?"

00:10:12.720 --> 00:10:13.680
"Thank you."

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:17.920
Even better, the fact that his character actually
has a point about the way regular people are

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:21.940
pushed down in an increasingly strange world
only adds to his impact.

00:10:21.940 --> 00:10:25.360
It's a brilliant, layered performance that's
going to be hard to top.

